course: technology
lesson: 21
title: Trains and the Underground
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A train is a long chain of carriages or wagons rolling on two steel rails. The rails steer the wheels, so the driver can only make the train go faster or slower; a train can never swerve round anything on the line.
explain: Steel wheels on smooth steel rails roll with very little friction, the rubbing that slows things down. That is why one locomotive, the engine that pulls the train, can pull a whole line of heavy wagons, and also why a fast train needs a very long way to stop.
explain: Stand on a platform when a fast train rushes through without stopping: the wind tugs at your coat and the train is past almost before you heard it coming. That is why you always wait behind the yellow line.
explain: To keep trains apart, the line is cut into sections, and a signal shows red while a train is in the section ahead. Under big cities, electric trains run in tunnels called the Underground, the Tube, the metro or the subway.
fact: Electric trains take their power from overhead wires or from a third rail beside the track; diesel trains carry their own engine.
fact: Because steel wheels roll so easily on steel rails, a fast train can need more than a kilometre to stop.
fact: Rails are fixed to sleepers laid on a bed of stones; at a level crossing, lights flash and barriers close the road before a train comes, and a second train may follow the first.
fact: A live third rail or an overhead wire can kill without any train in sight; overhead wires in Britain carry about 25,000 volts, and the electricity can jump to someone who only comes close.
fact: The London Underground opened in 1863 and was the first underground railway in the world.
fact: A signal shows red when a train is in the section ahead; modern signalling can stop a train automatically if it passes a red signal.
fact: Ticket barriers read paper tickets, cards or phones and then open; the gates close fast, so nobody should squeeze through behind someone else.
fact: High-speed trains run at around 300 km/h on smooth track with gentle curves, and their pointed noses help them slip through the air.
fact: Japan's Shinkansen bullet trains began in 1964 and are famous for running on time.
fact: In the cab the driver controls the speed with a lever and watches the signals; a safety pedal or button must be pressed regularly, or the train stops by itself. It is often called a dead man's handle.
fact: London's Docklands Light Railway has run with no driver in a cab since 1987; computers drive it between stations.
fact: Platform screen doors are glass walls along the platform whose doors open only when the train's doors line up with them, so nobody can fall onto the track.
fact: A freight train is a long line of wagons pulled by a strong locomotive, carrying coal, containers, cars or stone; one freight train can take dozens of lorries off the road.
fact: A usual passenger carriage runs on 8 wheels: two sets of 4 wheels, one set near each end.
exercise: Why can't a fast train stop quickly? | Steel wheels on steel rails have so little friction that it needs a long way to stop | Its brakes are only allowed to work at stations | Trains have no brakes and just roll until they slow down | The driver must ask the signal box before braking
exercise: Where do electric trains get their power? | From overhead wires or a third rail beside the track | From a big battery that is charged up at the depot once a week | From a diesel engine hidden under the floor of every carriage | From the steel rails being warmed up all day by the Sun
exercise: Who steers a train round a bend? | Nobody: the rails guide the wheels round the bend | The driver turns a big wheel in the cab, like a bus driver | The guard at the back pulls a long rope to swing the carriages | The signal box steers it round by radio from far away
exercise: Why is it dangerous to go near the railway line even when no train is coming? | A live rail or overhead wire can kill without a train | The stones are slippery and very sharp | The sleepers can sink under a child's weight and trap a foot | The rails are always too hot to touch
exercise: The barriers at a level crossing have just gone up after one train passed, but the lights start flashing again. What does it mean? | Another train is coming, so you wait | The crossing is broken and you can cross | The lights are being tested by a worker | It only matters for cars, not for people
exercise: When did the world's first underground railway open, in London? | 1863 | 1963 | 1763 | 1903
exercise: How do railway signals stop two trains from meeting? | The line is in sections and a signal shows red while a train is in the next one | Each driver phones the driver in front every minute | Trains are only allowed out at night | The rails tip a train off if another is near
exercise: What can modern signalling do if a train goes past a red signal? | Stop the train automatically | Turn the signal green at once | Nothing, it can only show colours | Send the train backwards to the station
exercise: How does a ticket barrier know your ticket is valid? | It reads the paper ticket, card or phone and then opens | A guard hidden behind a mirror checks every passenger's face | It weighs you as you walk up to it | It opens for anyone who pushes hard enough
exercise: Why is the front of a high-speed train pointed? | It helps the train slip through the air at around 300 km/h | It pushes cows off the line | It makes the train look like a bird, which children find exciting | It keeps rain off the driver's window
exercise: When did Japan's Shinkansen bullet trains begin? | 1964 | 1864 | 2004 | 1994
exercise: What happens if the driver stops pressing the safety pedal or button? | The train brakes and stops by itself | The train speeds up to reach the station | The lights in the carriages go out | The doors open so people can get out
exercise: Who drives the trains on London's Docklands Light Railway? | Computers, with no driver in a cab | A driver hidden in the last carriage | Passengers take turns at the front | A driver steers it by remote control from home
exercise: Why do some stations have glass walls with doors along the platform? | So nobody can fall onto the track, since they open only when the train is there | To keep the wind off the passengers' hair | To stop pigeons getting onto the trains | To make the station warmer in winter
exercise: Why send coal or containers by freight train instead of by lorry? | One freight train can take dozens of lorries off the road | Trains are the only things that are allowed by law to carry coal | Lorries are not allowed on motorways, so they can only use small roads | Freight trains never need any fuel or power, so they are free to run
exercise: A train has 6 carriages, and each carriage runs on 8 wheels. How many wheels? | 48 | 36 | 14 | 56
test: In a cartoon, a train swerves round a cow standing on the line. What is wrong? | A train can't swerve, because the rails steer it | Nothing, trains steer round things all the time | Only the driver's cab can swerve, not the carriages | Trains swerve only when they go slowly
test: «my bro says if theres no train coming the tracks are totally safe» What is right? | Wrong: a live rail or overhead wire can kill even with no train | Right: without a train coming there is no danger at all, so play away | Right, as long as you walk on the sleepers | Wrong, but only because the stones are sharp
test: What would happen if a train driver fainted at the controls? | He would stop pressing the safety pedal, so the train would brake | The train would keep going until it ran out of track | Passengers would have to take turns steering it to the next station | The signals would keep turning green to help
test: «ugh, a train went past already, barriers still down, can't we just duck under» What is the answer? | No, wait: a second train may be coming | Yes, one train has gone, so it is safe | Yes, if you run fast enough | Only if you can't hear anything
test: «mate squeezed through the gates behind me, free ride lol» What is the safety problem? | The gates close fast and can hit and trap a person | There is none, the gates are made of soft rubber and can't hurt | The gate will take money from both people's cards at once | The gate will lock the whole station until a guard comes
test: Ben says trains need long brakes because the wheels are made of rubber that slides. What is right? | No, the wheels are steel and roll so easily on steel rails that stopping takes long | Yes, rubber wheels slide on wet rails | Yes, and that is why trains slow down in rain only | No, trains could stop fast, but drivers like to brake slowly so nobody spills their drinks
test: «why can't they just go faster on the old wiggly line too» Why does a high-speed train need special track? | It needs smooth track with gentle curves to go around 300 km/h safely | Old lines are too narrow for its extra-wide wheels, so it would fall off the track | The old lines have no electricity at all | Fast trains can only run on bridges
test: You stand on a platform and a train is not stopping here. Where is the safe place? | Behind the yellow line, because the train is fast and its wind tugs | Right on the edge, so you can see it | Anywhere, because trains slow down at every station | On the track side of the line, so the driver can see you and wave back
test: «a train with no driver?? i'm not sitting at the front, it'll crash» What is right? | Computers drive it using signals from the track, and the DLR has done it since 1987 | Right, driverless trains are only tested with no people | Right, it only works in cartoons | Someone hides inside the front to drive it secretly
test: Count them: a freight train has 20 wagons, and each wagon carries 2 containers. How many containers? | 40 | 22 | 18 | 60
test: Which country's bullet trains are famous for being on time? | Japan | Brazil | Canada | Egypt
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a railway train requires a considerable distance in order to come to a halt. | The low friction between steel wheels and steel rails permits the train to roll with great ease, so that braking is protracted | Railway trains are not equipped with brakes of any description | Braking is permitted exclusively within the confines of a station | The rails are designed to accelerate any train that attempts to slow
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning electrified railway lines in the absence of any train. | A live rail or overhead conductor remains capable of inflicting fatal injury even when no train is present | Such lines are entirely harmless until a train approaches | The current is discontinued whenever the line is unoccupied | Only the stones beneath the rails present any hazard
exam: Ascertain the function of the device, frequently termed a dead man's handle, with which the driver's cab is furnished. | It halts the train automatically should the driver cease to operate it at regular intervals | It enables the driver to steer the train around obstructions | It permits passengers to communicate with the signal box | It accelerates the train automatically in the event of the driver becoming unwell, so as to reach a hospital
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of railway signals? | The line is divided into sections, and a signal displays red whilst the section ahead is occupied by a train | Each signal is changed at random so that drivers remain attentive | Signals serve solely to inform passengers of the time | A signal displays red only during the hours of darkness
